</article-title><source>Neuron.</source><volume>16</volume><year>1996</year><fpage>571</fpage><lpage>577</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8785054</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Wangetal1998a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>H.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Pan</surname><given-names>Z.</given-names></name><name><surname>Shi</surname><given-names>W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>B.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Wymore</surname><given-names>R.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Cohen</surname><given-names>I.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Dixon</surname><given-names>J.E.</given-names></name><name><surname>McKinnon</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>KCNQ2 and KCNQ3 potassium channel subunitsmolecular correlates of the M-channel</article-title><source>Science.</source><volume>282</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>1890</fpage><lpage>1893</lpage>a<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9836639</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Wangetal1998b"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Xia</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kass</surname><given-names>R.S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>MinK-KvLQT1 fusion proteins, evidence for multiple stoichiometries of the assembled IsK channel</article-title><source>J. Biol. Chem.</source><volume>273</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>34069</fpage><lpage>34074</lpage>b<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9852064</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Wickendenetal1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wickenden</surname><given-names>A.D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tsushima</surname><given-names>R.G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Losito</surname><given-names>V.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kaprielian</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Backx</surname><given-names>P.H.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effect of Cd<sup>2+</sup> on Kv4.2 and Kv1.4 expressed in <italic>Xenopus</italic> oocytes and on the transient outward currents in rat and rabbit ventricular myocytes</article-title><source>Cell Physiol Biochem.</source><volume>9</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>11</fpage><lpage>28</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10352341</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Yangetal1996"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yang</surname><given-names>N.</given-names></name><name><surname>George</surname><given-names>A.L.</given-names><suffix>Jr.</suffix></name><name><surname>Horn</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Molecular basis of charge movement in voltage-gated sodium channels</article-title><source>Neuron.</source><volume>16</volume><year>1996</year><fpage>113</fpage><lpage>122</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8562074</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="YangandSigworth1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yang</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Sigworth</surname><given-names>F.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Single-channel properties of <italic>I</italic><sub>Ks</sub> potassium channels</article-title><source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source><volume>112</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>665</fpage><lpage>678</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9834139</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Yellenetal1991"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yellen</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Jurman</surname><given-names>M.E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Abramson</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>MacKinnon</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mutations affecting internal TEA blockade identify the probable pore-forming region of a K<sup>+</sup> channel</article-title><source>Science.</source><volume>251</volume><year>1991</year><fpage>939</fpage><lpage>942</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2000494</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p><italic>Abbreviation used in this paper:</italic> CHO, Chinese hamster ovary.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>External TEA<sup>+</sup> sensitivity of K318I + V319Y and V319Y mutants of the human KCNQ1 channel in the absence and presence of KCNQ1. In A and B, traces are shown for cells transfected with the indicated constructs in response to voltage pulses to +60 mV before (&#x025cb;) and after (&#x02022;) 2-min rapid applications of extracellularly applied TEA<sup>+</sup>, and then after wash out (&#x025a1;). (A) The effects of 50 mM TEA<sup>+</sup> on currents measured in cells transfected with wild-type (WT) KCNQ1 with (bottom) and without (top) KCNQ1. Vertical bars represent 50 pA/pF for KCNQ1 alone, 100 pA/pF KCNE1 + KCNQ1, and horizontal bars represent 1 s. (B) The effects of 0.5 mM TEA<sup>+</sup> on current recorded from cells transfected with V319Y KCNQ1 with (bottom) and without (top) KCNE1. Bars represent 5 pA/pF for KCNQ1 alone, 50 pA/pF for KCNE1 + V319Y KCNQ1 and 1 s. (C) Dose&#x02013;response curves of external TEA<sup>+</sup>. Currents measured in each [TEA<sup>+</sup>] were normalized to currents in the absence of TEA<sup>+</sup> and plotted versus [TEA<sup>+</sup>]. Plotted are mean data &#x000b1; SEM. The number of cells studied is indicated in parentheses. Symbols are as follows: WT KCNQ1 alone (&#x025aa;)&#x0003b; WT KCNQ1 + KCNE1 (&#x025a1;)&#x0003b; K318I + V319Y KCNQ1 alone (&#x02022;)&#x0003b; K318I + V319Y KCNQ1 + KCNE1 (&#x025cb;)&#x0003b; V319Y KCNQ1 alone (&#x025b4;)&#x0003b; and V319Y KCNQ1 + KCNE1 (&#x025b5;).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8275.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>The effects of 2 mM of external Cd<sup>2+</sup> on <italic>I</italic><sub>Ks</sub> channels coexpressed with wild-type or mutated (F54C) KCNE1. The figure shows typical traces recorded in response to 2-s pulses to +60 mV in the absence (&#x025cb;) and presence (&#x02022;) of 2 mM (extracellular) Cd<sup>2+</sup> and then after wash out (&#x025a1;). (A) WT KCNQ1 + WT KCNE1. Bars represent 2 nA and 1 s. (B) WT KCNQ1 + F54C KCNE1. Bar: 2 nA. (C) V319Y KCNQ1 + WT KCNE1&#x0003b; (D) V319Y KCNQ1 + F54C KCNE1&#x0003b; and (E) V319Y KCNQ1 + G55C KCNE1. Bars: (C&#x02013;E) 0.2 nA.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8275.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Rapid application of [TEA<sup>+</sup>]<sub>o</sub> (10 mM) blocks V319Y KCNQ1 + F54C KCNE1 channels. Currents were recorded by the same voltage protocol as in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">Fig. 2</xref>&#x0003b; and traces with (&#x02022;) or without (&#x025cb;) application of external TEA<sup>+</sup> were superimposed. The duration of the rapid application of TEA<sup>+</sup> was indicated as the white bar below the traces. Bars represent 0.5 nA and 1 s.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8275.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Typical recordings of the effects of external Cd<sup>2+</sup> on V319Y KCNQ1 + F54C KCNE1 channels in the absence and presence of [TEA<sup>+</sup>]<sub>o</sub> (10 mM). The currents were elicited by 2-s pulses to +60 mV every 15 s from a &#x02212;60-mV holding potential. (A) The experimental procedure is shown at a slow time base. The application of Cd<sup>2+</sup> and/or TEA<sup>+</sup> is indicated as the bars above the current traces. The time scale is shown as the time after the rupture of the membrane. The traces shown in B and C are marked by an open circle (before Cd<sup>2+</sup>), closed circle (after Cd<sup>2+</sup>), and an open square (wash out). Current traces from A are displayed at a faster time base for the application of Cd<sup>2+</sup> alone (B) and for the application of Cd<sup>2+</sup> in the presence of 10 mM TEA<sup>+</sup> (C). Bars represent 2 nA and 1 s.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8275.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Summary of the effects of external Cd<sup>2+</sup> on TEA<sup>+</sup>-sensitive constructs. The percentage of remaining current after a 30-s application of 2 mM Cd<sup>2+</sup> was normalized to current before Cd<sup>2+</sup> application. The currents were elicited by the same voltage protocol as that in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F4">Fig. 4</xref>. White column shows block of V319Y KCNQ1 + WT KCNE1 constructs without TEA<sup>+</sup> (<italic>n</italic> = 8). Gray column shows block of V319Y KCNQ1 + F54C KCNE1 constructs without TEA<sup>+</sup> (<italic>n</italic> = 12). Hatched column shows block for V319Y KCNQ1 + F54C KCNE1 constructs in the presence of 10 mM TEA<sup>+</sup> outside (<italic>n</italic> = 6) measuring the data according to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F4">Fig. 4</xref> A.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8275.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="S1"><label>Scheme S1</label><graphic xlink:href="JGP8275.s1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Kinetics of Cd<sup>2+</sup> block of V319Y KCNQ1 + F54C KCNE1 channels. Currents were measured by the same voltage protocol as that in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F4">Fig. 4</xref>. (A) Onset of block. Superimposed traces (top) recorded before (&#x025cb;) and once every 15 s after the application of Cd<sup>2+</sup>(2 mM). Bars represent 0.5 nA and 1 s. The bottom panel shows averaged plot of currents measured at the end of each depolarizing voltage pulse normalized to control, pre-Cd<sup>2+</sup> current, plotted versus time after Cd<sup>2+</sup> application in the absence (&#x025cb;) and presence (&#x02022;) of 2 mM TEA<sup>+</sup>. The dashed curves are the best fit single-exponential function to the data (&#x003c4; = 12.8 s [&#x025cb;] and 16.4 s [&#x02022;]). (B) Recovery from Cd<sup>2+</sup> block. The top panel shows current block measured every 15 s after washing out Cd<sup>2+</sup> (2 mM) as indicated by solid bar in the figure. The trace marked by the asterisk is the first measured after washing out Cd<sup>2+</sup>. Bars represent 0.4 nA and 1 s. The bottom panel shows the averaged plot of currents normalized end of pulse current versus time after wash out of Cd<sup>2+</sup> in the absence (&#x025cb;) and presence (&#x02022;) of 2 mM TEA<sup>+</sup>, and the dashed curves are the best fit single-exponential function (&#x003c4; = 19.8 s [&#x025cb;] and 22.6 s [&#x02022;]) to the data.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8275.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Kinetics of Cd<sup>2+</sup> block of V319Y KCNQ1 + G55C KCNE1 channels. The experimental protocol and format of the plots are same as that in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F6">Fig. 6</xref>. (A) Onset of Cd<sup>2+</sup>(2 mM) block. Superimposed traces (top) recorded before (&#x025cb;) and once every 15 s after the Cd<sup>2+</sup> application. In the presence of 2 mM TEA<sup>+</sup>, the trace before the Cd<sup>2+</sup> application is marked by an arrowhead. Bars represent 0.5 nA and 1 s. In the bottom panel, the dashed curves are the best fit single-exponential function to the data (&#x003c4; = 17.2 s [&#x025cb;, control], 17.3 s [&#x02022;, 2 mM TEA<sup>+</sup>]). (B) Recovery from 2 mM Cd<sup>2+</sup> block. Bars for superimposed traces represent 0.5 nA and 1 s. In the bottom panel, the dashed curves are the best fit single-exponential function to the data (&#x003c4; = 25.2 s [&#x025cb;, control], 28.1 s [&#x02022;, 2 mM TEA<sup>+</sup>]).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8275.f7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>TEA<sup>+</sup> does not alter the kinetics of Cd<sup>2+</sup> (10 mM) block of V319Y KCNQ1 + F54C KCNE1 channels. (A) Currents are shown recorded at a slow time base illustrating the onset of Cd<sup>2+</sup> block of channel activity in the absence (left) and presence (right) of TEA<sup>+</sup> (2 mM). The application of the test compounds is illustrated as bars above the current traces, which were recorded continuously in the same cell. The time scale indicates the time after the rupture of the membrane to establish whole-cell recording conditions, and the broken axis reflects the period in which Cd<sup>2+</sup> was washed out after the first exposure. (B) Superposition of current traces recorded every 30 s during the onset of Cd<sup>2+</sup> block. Symbols or asterisks in A mark the traces that are shown in B. Bars represent 0.25 nA and 1 s. In the inset of right panel, the traces in the presence of TEA<sup>+</sup> are plotted at higher gain as indicated by the same arrows. In this case, the bars represent 50 pA and 1 s. (C) Averaged plots are shown in the same format in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F6">Fig. 6</xref> A in the absence (&#x025cb;, &#x003c4; = 15.4 s) and presence of TEA at 0.1 mM (&#x025aa;, &#x003c4; = 13.2 s) and 0.5 mM (&#x02022;, &#x003c4; = 14.6 s) and 2 mM (&#x025b4;, &#x003c4; = 17.8 s). (D) Plotted are the expected (&#x025a1;) and measured (&#x025aa;) ratios of time constant of Cd<sup>2+</sup> (10 mM) block in presence (&#x003c4;) and absence (&#x003c4;<sub>o)</sub> of TEA<sup>+</sup> (0.1 mM&#x0003b; <italic>n</italic> = 3, 0.5 mM&#x0003b; <italic>n</italic> = 3, 2 mM&#x0003b; <italic>n</italic> = 5). In each case, <italic>f</italic><sub>UB</sub> (the fraction of unbound channels) was measured. The estimated ratio &#x003c4;/&#x003c4;<sub>o</sub> was determined using the mean <italic>f</italic><sub>UB</sub> value (0.182) and <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="FD4"/>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8275.f8"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>